e absent, he is absent, we are absent, you[pl.] are absent, they are absent, to be absent.\par 12.\emdash  One man, one mother, one cup; two men, two mothers, three cups.\par \par NOTES\par 1. }{\b\f210 N}{\f208 e}{\b\f210 scio }{\f208 u}{\b\f210 bi sint}{\f208 : Just notice that we have }{\b\f210 sint}{\f208  and not }{\b\f210 sunt}{\f208 : you will know why later.\par 2. }{\b\f210 D}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 mi}{\f208 . We have seen (L 2, P 3) }{\b\f210 d}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 mum}{\f208  }{\b\f210 v}{\f208 e}{\b\f210 nis}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 you are coming home}{\f208 . }{\i\f211 You are at home}{\f208  is actually }{\b\f210 d}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 mi}{\f208  a}{\b\f210 des}{\f208 : let's be content for now to notice that the form of the adverbial of place changes depending on whether it is about}{\i\f211  the place where one is}{\f208  or about }{\i\f211 the place where (whither) one goes}{\f208 ; or if you prefer, in accordance with whether there is or there is not }{\i\f211 movement}{\f208 . To remember thismore easily, think of that M in }{\b\f210 d}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 mum}{\f208  as corresponding to the Movement.\par 3. Au}{\b\f210 tem}{\f208  and }{\b\f210 sed}{\f208  have almost the same sense, but are not put in the same position in the sentence. You must have by now noticed that the position of the words is not always the same in Latin as in English. In particular, do not seek an absolute rule which will enable you to perfectly position the words of a sentence, because various combinations are possible, which usage will teach you gradually. Simply remember that the subject, verb, and complements, are not aways where you might expect them. If you have already done German, you are almost saved: the German construction follows the same principles a bit as the Latin construction.\par 4. A}{\b\f210 bsum}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 I am not there}{\f208 ; a}{\b\f210 dsum}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 I am there}{\f208 ; as well as }{\b\f210 p}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 ssum}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 I can}{\f208 ; are conjugated in the same way as }{\b\f210 sum}{\f208 . This conjugation differs significantly from that of }{\b\f210 l}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 quor}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 I speak}{\f208  (L 1, P 7), which itself differs from the conjugation of }{\b\f210 do}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 I give}{\f208  (L 2, P 9). Reassure yourself: the other forms of conjugation which we shall see later will be increasingly easy to learn.\par 5. U}{\b\f210 nus}{\f208 , feminine u}{\b\f210 na}{\f208 , neuter u}{\b\f210 num}{\f208 ; }{\b\f210 d}{\f208 u}{\b\f210 o}{\f208 , f. }{\b\f210 d}{\f208 u}{\b\f210 ae}{\f208 ; n. }{\b\f210 d}{\f208 u}{\b\f210 o}{\f208 ; }{\b\f210 tres}{\f208 , f. }{\b\f210 tres}{\f208 , n. }{\b\f210 tr}{\f208 i}{\b\f210 a}{\f208\lang1036 . }{\f208 In Latin, there are three genders: masculine, feminine, and neuter. The first corresponds }{\i\f211 approximately}{\f208  to the men and to the male animals, the second to the women and to the female animals, and the third to those things for which it is impossible to allocate a sex. That is why the majority of adjectives take three different forms to be able to agree with the nouns to which they relate.\par }{\b\f210 H}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 mo}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 a man}{\f208 , }{\b\f210 h}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 mines}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 men}{\f208 . Similarly, }{\b\f210 s}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 ror}{\f208 , }{\b\f210 sor}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 res}{\f208 ; }{\b\f210 m}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 ter}{\f208 , }{\b\f210 m}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 tres}{\f208 ; }{\b\f210 fr}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 ter}{\f208 , }{\b\f210 fr}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 tres}{\f208 . This formation of the plural corresponds to the English plural in S (a brother; brothers). However it is not the only possible form; see for instance: u}{\b\f210 num p}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 culum}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 a cup}{\f208 ; }{\b\f210 d}{\f208 u}{\b\f210 o p}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 cula}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 two cups}{\f208 .\par Do not seek to memorize these various formations of the plural, and save all your energy to try and remember the forms of the }{\i\f211 full sentences}{\f208 .\par \par EXERCITATIO\par \par 1.\emdash  Three men are at home.\par 2.\emdash  (}{\i\f211 Only}{\f208 )-one man comes home.\par 3.\emdash  Do you[sg.] see my brothers?\par 4.\emdash  Your[sg.] sisters can speak Italian (}{\i\f211 in-Italian to-speak they-can}{\f208 ).\par 5.\emdash  I do not understand what you say (}{\i\f211 what you[sg.]-say not I-understand}{\f208 ).\par 6.\emdash  Can you[sg.] help me (}{\i\f211 to-me for-assistance to-be}{\f208 )?\par 7.\emdash  My sister can be of no help to you[sg.].\par \par 1. Do not try to understand yet why we have here }{\b\f210 d}{\f208 i}{\b\f210 cas}{\f208  and not }{\b\f210 d}{\f208 i}{\b\f210 cis}{\f208  as in L 1, P 6.}{\par }}